____________________________________________________________________________________________________
FINAL STEADY STATE (no absorbing barrier)
____________________________________________________________________________________________________

J N = R F without a barrier: under continuous formation, after the stabilized population has reached its own
steady state, the yields of every exit, chemical activation and thermal reaction together (GO10 eqs. 7-9).

Not available: T = 298 K, p = 200.72 Torr: relative residual 1.0184981772730757e-1 of J N = F exceeds the tolerance 1e-8. In the final steady state this means that J is numerically singular: the thermal rate coefficient of a well without a bimolecular sink is negligible compared with the collision frequency (the final steady state is reached only after about 0.1/k_uni, O02 text after eq. 13); use the intermediate steady state for such conditions.
____________________________________________________________________________________________________
THERMAL RATE COEFFICIENTS OF THE FINAL STEADY STATE (lowest eigenpair of J)
____________________________________________________________________________________________________

k_uni = sum of the specific rate coefficients averaged over the normalized eigenvector of the lowest eigenvalue
lambda_1 of J (Gonzalez-Garcia, Olzmann, PCCP 12, 12290 (2010), text after eq. 12); lambda_1 = k_uni in exact
arithmetic (eq. 12), checked as the sum rule (warning above 1.5e-2). Eigen-solver: InverseIteration. The thermal branching is
k_th(channel)/k_uni: the yields of the thermal decay of the network.

Warning, T = 298 K, p = 200.72 torr: sum rule lambda_1 = k_uni violated: lambda_1 = 1.0228222737142245e-6 s-1, k_uni = 9.743302791967625e-7 s-1, relative deviation 4.977e-2 > 1.5e-2. k_uni is the average of the specific rate coefficients over the normalized thermal eigenvector (Gonzalez-Garcia, Olzmann, Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys. 12, 12290 (2010), text after eq. 12), equal to the lowest eigenvalue lambda_1 (eq. 12) in exact arithmetic. Here lambda_1 is not resolved in double precision (it lies far below the collision frequency and the largest k(E)); the eigenvector, and with it k_uni, is much less sensitive. What to do: use k_uni, and confirm it with the inverse iteration (the most accurate solver for the thermal eigenvector); if the solvers give different k_uni, the condition is beyond double precision.

